  "_note": "NEW task state file created from program-design/runtime/problem_state_template.json for task 2025A (Acoustic Preservation of the Electric Sasando). No content carried over from any previously worked example.",
  "task": {
    "problem_id": "2025A_Sasando",
    "description": "A traditional Indonesian sasando string instrument uses a dried-lontar-leaf resonator (hand-formed) that acoustically amplifies frequencies between 98 Hz and 1047 Hz. The strings are mounted on a bamboo tube 70 cm long, 8 cm diameter. Modern electric versions replace the resonator with a pickup+speaker, sacrificing acoustic quality. Task: develop a method for the electric sasando to replicate the authentic acoustic timbre of the traditional instrument.",
    "target_quantity": "A concrete, physically-justified signal-processing/acoustic-design method (equivalently, an impedance/filter response H(f) and its physical parameters) that re-imposes on the electric string's electrical signal the traditional resonator's frequency-response signature over 98-1047 Hz, so the speaker output sounds like the acoustic sasando.",
    "safety_constraints_ref": "No structural safety constraint; acceptance criteria are physical: (1) the modeled resonator's acoustic response must be physically consistent (correct ODE form, correct boundary conditions, positive physical constants), (2) its predicted characteristic resonant frequency/frequencies must be order-of-magnitude consistent with the stated 98-1047 Hz operating band (not wildly outside it), (3) any external data used must be genuinely independent for verification vs. modeling."
  },
  "session_key": "2da33c6e-d45f-4d5d-b8c8-6f21193b80b9",
  "stage": "node_1_spec",
  "quota": {
    "downstream_retries_remaining": 3,
    "note": "Decrement once per code-edit + re-run cycle inside node_2b_modeling; at 0 with no convergence, trigger annealing rollback to hypothesis_layer."
  },
  "hypothesis_layer": {
    "model_form": "Tentative (Node 2a to confirm/replace): model the dried lontar-leaf resonator as a lightweight 2D thin circular plate (radius ~4 cm, matching the bamboo tube's diameter, with the tube acting as the plate's supporting rim) driven axially by the transverse string vibration at one/two localized contact points (the bamboo tube's end or contact points). Governing equation: 2D plate theory, flexural rigidity D = E h^3 / (12(1-nu^2)), free or clamped edge depending on how the leaf sits in the tube, forced response solved via modal expansion (circular-plate Bessel-function eigenmodes) to obtain the frequency-dependent gain H(f) = sum of damped harmonic-oscillator terms; this H(f) is the EQ curve the electric sasando's DSP must re-apply to the pickup signal.",
    "assumptions": {
      "resonator_geometry": "circular plate, radius ~0.04 m (assumed equal to half the bamboo tube's 8 cm diameter - candidate, to be justified or revised in Node 2a, since the actual leaf resonator's real size is not given in the problem)",
      "plate_thickness_m": "candidate ~0.0005 m (0.5 mm), a typical dried-leaf order of magnitude, flagged as ASSUMED this run, not measured data",
      "elastic_modulus_Pa": "candidate ~1e9 Pa for dried plant fiber (order-of-magnitude literature value, flagged as candidate, to be confirmed or dropped in Node 1.5/2a, not a settled input)",
      "density_kg_m3": "candidate ~1000-1500 for dried plant matter + surrounding air coupling, flagged as candidate",
      "edge_condition": "clamped (tube rim) vs free vs simply-supported: mechanism-relevant choice, to be decided in Node 2a's mechanism-match check, not assumed away",
      "damping_model": "damped-harmonic-oscillator (Lorentzian) modal shape per mode, damping ratio candidate ~0.01-0.05 (very lightly damped, consistent with a resonant, 'zingy' plucked-string resonance) - flagged as candidate"
    },
    "last_known_good_version": null
  },
  "dimensional_table": {
    "_note": "Will be finalized at Node 2b when the governing equation(s) are written out; placeholder structure only.",
    "h": "length",
    "R": "length",
    "D_flex": "force * length",
    "p": "pressure",
    "z": "length",
    "t": "time",
    "rho_air": "density",
    "f": "frequency"
  },
  "internal_prior": {
    "_note": "Private Node-1a intuition sketch ONLY. Not evidence. Never cited in the report or shown to the user. The only permitted later use is a conscious divergence check against Node 2b's gate-verified result, logged as an internal_prior_divergence audit record if it is large.",
    "sketch": "Regime: forced 2D elastic plate vibration, NOT a classic open-pipe / Helmholtz air-cavity problem (though air-loading of the leaf is a secondary correction, likely small at these low plate-mass scales). A thin, light, circular dried-leaf plate clamped around its rim (roughly the bamboo tube's diameter, ~4 cm radius) will have a (0,0)-like axisymmetric bending fundamental mode at a frequency that scales like f1 ~ (k^2 * D / (rho_s * h * R^4)), where k is a mode-shape constant, D is flexural rigidity ~ E h^3/(12(1-nu^2)), rho_s is in-plane (surface) mass density. With E ~ 1e9 Pa, h ~ 0.0005 m, R ~ 0.04 m, rho_s ~ rho_vol*h ~ 1200*0.0005 = 0.6 kg/m^2: D ~ 1e9*(0.0005^3)/(12*0.9) ~ 1.1e-5 N*m (order), so f1 ~ sqrt-ish combination lands roughly in the 100-400 Hz range - i.e. plausibly INSIDE the stated 98-1047 Hz band, suggesting the fundamental bending mode of the leaf sits at the LOWER end of that band, with higher overtones (2,0; 3,0; 0,1 etc. circular plate modes) stacking up through ~1 kHz, matching the 'resonates across the whole 98-1047 Hz range' description as a multi-modal, peaky response rather than a single flat band. This means the electric sasando's DSP re-synthesis is NOT a single narrow peak but a sum of several damped-oscillator (Lorentzian) peaks at the circular-plate eigenfrequencies, weighted by which modes are actually excited by the string's excitation geometry (off-center vs centered contact). If the true leaf is much thinner or much stiffer than my candidate constants, f1 could shift by 2-3x, so order-of-magnitude, not exact, agreement with 98 Hz is the honest prior."
  },
